        {
          "question_number": 1,
          "question_text": "What is the formal limit definition of the derivative $f'(x)$?",
          "marks": 1,
          "options": [
            {
              "letter": "A",
              "text": "$\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) + f(x)}{h}$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "B",
              "text": "$\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "C",
              "text": "$\\lim_{h \\to \\infty} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "D",
              "text": "$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{x}$"
            }
          ],
          "correct_answer": "B",
          "explanation": "The derivative measures the instantaneous rate of change and is defined as the limit of the difference quotient as $h$ approaches zero."
        },
        {
          "question_number": 2,
          "question_text": "Which theorem describes the relationship between differentiability and continuity?",
          "marks": 1,
          "options": [
            {
              "letter": "A",
              "text": "If $f(x)$ is continuous, it must be differentiable."
            },
            {
              "letter": "B",
              "text": "If $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=a$, then $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=a$."
            },
            {
              "letter": "C",
              "text": "Differentiability and continuity are unrelated."
            },
            {
              "letter": "D",
              "text": "A function is differentiable only if it is a constant."
            }
          ],
          "correct_answer": "B",
          "explanation": "Differentiability is a stronger condition than continuity; every differentiable function is continuous, but not every continuous function is differentiable."
        },
        {
          "question_number": 3,
          "question_text": "What is the derivative of $f(x) = e^x - x$?",
          "marks": 1,
          "options": [
            {
              "letter": "A",
              "text": "$e^x$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "B",
              "text": "$e^x - 1$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "C",
              "text": "$xe^{x-1} - 1$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "D",
              "text": "$e^x + 1$"
            }
          ],
          "correct_answer": "B",
          "explanation": "Using the difference rule, the derivative of $e^x$ is $e^x$ and the derivative of $x$ is $1$."
        },
        {
          "question_number": 4,
          "question_text": "Using the Product Rule, find the derivative of $f(x) = x e^x$.",
          "marks": 1,
          "options": [
            {
              "letter": "A",
              "text": "$e^x$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "B",
              "text": "$x e^x$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "C",
              "text": "$(x+1)e^x$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "D",
              "text": "$x^2 e^x$"
            }
          ],
          "correct_answer": "C",
          "explanation": "By the product rule: $\\frac{d}{dx}[x e^x] = x \\frac{d}{dx}[e^x] + e^x \\frac{d}{dx}[x] = x e^x + e^x(1) = (x+1)e^x$."
        },
        {
          "question_number": 5,
          "question_text": "What is the derivative of $y = \\cos(x^3)$ using the Chain Rule?",
          "marks": 1,
          "options": [
            {
              "letter": "A",
              "text": "$-3x^2 \\sin(x^3)$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "B",
              "text": "$\\sin(x^3)$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "C",
              "text": "$-3x^2 \\cos(x^3)$"
            },
            {
              "letter": "D",
              "text": "$- \\sin(3x^2)$"
            }
          ],
          "correct_answer": "A",
          "explanation": "Let $u = x^3$. Then $y = \\cos(u)$. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} = -\\sin(u) \\cdot 3x^2 = -3x^2 \\sin(x^3)$."
        }
      ]
    }
